# tab_main

**Claim carried:** C2/C4: width and coverage of every arm per dataset, and the headline arm's paired width ratio to both PPI++ baselines with its pre-fixed class and the floor in two units.

**Shows:** one row per dataset at its headline n; first block: cell = mean width in each dataset's own units (empirical coverage %), dagger = misses the win rule; second block: paired width ratio with 95% CI of PPI++ with the unclipped tuning (the calibrated spline arm is in tab_headline_full), and of the selected interval to the package PPI++ (`ppi-python` 0.2.3, clips the estimated tuning to [0, 1]), to the unclipped PPI++ and to the strongest valid baseline of the two, each with its class under the margin fixed before the runs (none = CI inside [0.98, 1.02]; gain = CI upper bound below 0.95 and valid; small = otherwise; classes from headline.csv, checked against the ratio in cells.csv); floor/PPI++ = estimated within-class floor as the ratio of the standard deviations (asympt.) and in measured-width units (meas.), neither a bound. Two stacked blocks in one table*, centred; use with \footnotesize. Strongest valid baseline (Outline rev. 9): in each cell the narrower of the package PPI++ (P) and the unclipped PPI++ (U) among those whose coverage passes the rule; a double dagger means neither passes and the narrower of the two is used (3 of 9 cells: alphafold, census income, forest). The narrower of the two regardless of validity (`ratio_vs_best_ppipp`) gives the same class in all 9 cells. The columns Ratio, with X (T2 cells only) are ratios to the package PPI++ with its class; a double dagger there marks the T2 cells in which neither baseline passes the coverage rule (2 of 4: alphafold, census income); against the strongest valid baseline their ratios are alphafold 1.004, census educ. 1.009, census health 1.004, census income 1.009, all classed no gain.

**How to read:** ratio below 1 with CI below 1 = shorter than PPI++; coverage is read next to width so a short interval that under-covers is visible.

